8.3 MkV: conditioning on variable characters
Tells the model that all traits are expected to have at least one character varing. It also excludes invariant characters from the likelihood, since empirical matrices are curated to only include variable traits.

RevBayesMrBayesBEAST2 (MM)


Add coding="variable" to the CTMC:
seq ~ dnPhyloCTMC(tree=phylogeny, Q=Q_morpho, type="Standard", coding="variable")
seq.clamp(morpho)
Constant characters will be removed automatically before the inference starts.


lset applyto = (1) coding = variable rates = gamma
Constant characters are excluded automatically once coding = variable is set, but MrBayes will not remove them from the matrix for you — if your input file still contains constant characters, either let coding = variable handle it during likelihood calculation, or explicitly exclude them from the matrix first:
exclude 7 31 61 83 107


MkV support is provided by the same LewisMK substitution model as plain Mk — the difference lives in the alignment, not the substitution model. BEAST2 implements the ascertainment correction by setting ascertained="true" on the alignment and telling it which columns represent the “all constant states” categories to correct for (via excludefrom/excludeto), rather than removing invariant characters from the matrix directly:
&lt;data id="morph1" spec="FilteredAlignment" data="@morphAlignment"
      filter="1-10" ascertained="true" excludefrom="10" excludeto="12"&gt;
    &lt;userDataType id="morphDataType.morph1" spec="StandardData" nrOfStates="3"/&gt;
&lt;/data&gt;

&lt;siteModel id="morphSiteModel.s:morph1" spec="SiteModel"&gt;
    &lt;substModel id="LewisMK.s:morph1" spec="LewisMK"
                datatype="@morphDataType.morph1"/&gt;
&lt;/siteModel&gt;
In BEAUti, this is what “condition on coding only variable characters” sets up for you when adding morphological data, you don’t need to hand-edit excludefrom/excludeto yourself in the normal workflow, but it’s worth knowing what’s actually happening underneath, since a mismatched excludefrom/excludeto range relative to your actual matrix is a known source of NaN likelihoods and initialization failures if you ever do end up editing this block by hand.
